generate over F, and for each such F, this stronger bound is always\n\nattained for some d by Lemma 4.2 (except when F=F 4, over which there is\n\nno nondegenerate d). When F is a prime ﬁeld F p and d is nondegenerate over\n\nF(which requires p≥5), Theorem 5.1 gives upper bounds of V F,d ≤1/2\n\nwhen p≡1 (mod 4), and V F,d ≤ ⌈(p−1)/3⌉/(p−1) when p≡3 (mod 4),\n\nand Lemmata 5.5–5.8 show that these bounds are always met for some d in\n\nevery such F.\n\nSo it remains to determine precisely how high V F,d can be when [F:F p]\n\nis neither a power of 2 nor a multiple of 3. If ℓ is the least odd prime\n\ndivisor of [F:F p], then Lemma 3.2 shows that there is some d such that\n\nV F,d =ℓ+1\n\n2 ℓ[F:F p]. We are unaware of any pair (F, d) where this value is\n\nexceeded. Thus we make the following conjecture that the value of V F,d\n\nobserved in Lemma 3.2 is in fact the upper bound.\n\nConjecture 6.1 (Upper Bound Conjecture).Suppose that [F:F p]is not a\n\npower of 2, and let ℓ be the least odd prime divisor of [F:F p]. Let d be a\n\npositive integer with gcd(d, q −1) = 1 that is not degenerate over F. Then\n\nV F,d ≤ℓ+1\n\n2 ℓ[F:F p].\n\nThis bound coincides with the universal bound V F,d ≤(2/3)[F:F p] when\n\nℓ= 3, but is often stronger when ℓ > 3. Computer checks have veriﬁed\n\nConjecture 6.1 for all ﬁelds F of order less than 10 13 .\n\nAgain consider our universal bound V F,d ≤(2/3)[F:F p] for d nondegen-\n\nerate over F. It is interesting that the proof (in Section 3) does not use\n\nStickelberger’s Theorem (which underlies Lemma 2.9). Attempts to prove\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Jr_V in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AJr_V+in%3Amath)\nciteturn10search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday;   * The unbroken spectrum of type-A Frobenius seaweeds ... ~Coll Jr.~and Matthew Hyatt and Colton Magnant}, title = {{T}he unbroken spectrum of type-{A} {F}robenius seaweeds}, year = {2016}, eprint = {1606.05397}, note = {arXiv:1606.05397v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\n  * The unbroken spectrum of type-A Frobenius seaweeds\n\nVincent E. Coll Jr., Matthew Hyatt, Colton Magnant\n\nJun 20 2016 math.RA arXiv:1606.05397v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{1606.05397, author = {Vincent E.~Coll Jr.~and Matthew Hyatt and Colton Magnant}, title = {{T}he unbroken spectrum of type-{A} {F}robenius seaweeds}, year = {2016}, eprint = {1606.05397}, note = {arXiv:1606.05397v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nIf $\\mathfrak{g}$ is a Frobenius Lie algebra, then for certain $F\\in \\mathfrak{g}^*$ the natural map $\\mathfrak{g}\\longrightarrow \\mathfrak{g}^* $ given by $x \\longmapsto F[x,-]$ is an isomorphism. The inverse image of $F$ under this isomorphism is called a principal element. We show that if $\\mathfrak{g}$ is a Frobenius seaweed subalgebra of $A_{n-1}=\\mathfrak{sl}(n)$ then the spectrum of the adjoint of a principal element consists of an unbroken set of integers whose multiplicities have a symmetric distribution. Our proof methods are constructive and combinatorial in nature.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe index and spectrum of Lie poset algebras of types B, C, and D - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/The_index_and_spectrum_of_Lie_poset_algebras_of_types_B%2C_C%2C_and_D)\nciteturn10search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago;   * The unbroken spectrum of type-A Frobenius seaweeds ... This page was last edited on 6 May 2026, at 12:52.\n  * On seaweed subalgebras and meander graphs in type \\(\\mathsf{C}\\)\n  * On semi-invariants and index for biparabolic (seaweed) algebras. I\n  * Quotients of Coxeter complexes and 𝑃-partitions\n  * Signed posets\n  * Symplectic meanders\n  * The index of Lie poset algebras\n  * The index of nilpotent Lie poset algebras\n  * The principal element of a Frobenius Lie algebra\n  * The unbroken spectrum of type-A Frobenius seaweeds\n\nCited in\n\n(8)\n\n  * The index of Lie poset algebras\n  * Toral posets and the binary spectrum property\n  * Classification of contact seaweeds\n  * The index of nilpotent Lie poset algebras\n  * Hyperoctahedral group characters and a type-BC analog of graph coloring\n  * The evolution of the spectrum of a Frobenius Lie algebra under deformation\n  * The index and spectrum of Lie poset algebras of type B, C, and D\n  * On toral posets and contact Lie algebras\n\nThis page was built for publication: The index and spectrum of Lie poset algebras of types B, C, and D\n\nReport a bug (only for logged in users!)Click here to report a bug for this page (MaRDI item Q2049623)\n\nRetrieved from \"https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/w/index.php?title=The_index_and_spectrum_of_Lie_poset_algebras_of_types_B,_C,_and_D&oldid=78138206\"\n\nThis page was last edited on 6 May 2026, at 12:52. Warning: Page may not contain recent updates.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFinite Simple Groups Research Papers - Academia.edu (https://www.academia.edu/Documents/in/Finite_Simple_Groups?page=first)\nciteturn10search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; The unbroken spectrum of type-A Frobenius seaweeds ... 2024, Journal of Algebraic Combinatorics\n\ndescriptionView Paperarrow_downwardDownload\n\nDirac inequality for highest weight Harish-Chandra modules II\n\nby Vít Tuček\n\n2024, arXiv (Cornell University)\n\nLet G be a connected simply connected noncompact exceptional simple Lie group of Hermitian type. In this paper, we work with the Dirac inequality which is a very useful tool for the classification of unitary highest weight modules.\n\ndescriptionView Paperarrow_downwardDownload\n\nThe unbroken spectrum of type-A Frobenius seaweeds\n\nby Vincent Coll\n\n2024, Journal of Algebraic Combinatorics\n\nIf g is a Frobenius Lie algebra, then for certain F ∈ g * the natural map g −→ g * given by x −→ F [x, −] is an isomorphism. The inverse image of F under this isomorphism is called a principal element. We show that if g is a Frobenius... more\n\nIf g is a Frobenius Lie algebra, then for certain F ∈ g * the natural map g −→ g * given by x −→ F [x, −] is an isomorphism. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnhancements of link colorings via idempotents of quandle rings (https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.09257)\nciteturn10academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.3 years ago; We show that quandle rings and their idempotents lead to proper enhancements of the well-known quandle coloring invariant of links in the 3-space. ... The data supports our conjecture about triviality of idempotents of integral quandle rings of finite latin quandles.\nTitle: Enhancements of link colorings via idempotents of quandle rings\nAuthors: Mohamed Elhamdadi, Brandon Nunez, Mahender Singh\nDate: Tue Jul 19 13:16:22 2022\n\nWe show that quandle rings and their idempotents lead to proper enhancements of the well-known quandle coloring invariant of links in the 3-space. We give explicit examples to show that the new invariants are also stronger than the $\\Hom$ quandle invariant when the coloring quandles are medial. We provide computer assisted computations of idempotents for all quandles of order less than six, and also determine the ones for which the set of all idempotents is itself a quandle. The data supports our conjecture about triviality of idempotents of integral quandle rings of finite latin quandles. We also determine Peirce spectra for complex quandle algebras of some small order quandles.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe index of Lie poset algebras | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/346700437_The_index_of_Lie_poset_algebras)\nciteturn10search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; In the case of Frobenius seaweed subalgebras of $A_{n-1}=\\mathfrak{sl}(n)$, or type-A seaweeds for short, it has been shown that the spectrum can be computed combinatorially using an attendant graph. With the aid of such graphs, it was further shown that the spectrum of a type-A seaweed consists of an unbroken sequence of integers centered at $\\frac{1}{2}$.\n... Now, one can show that type-A seaweeds of the form p A 1|2|···|2 2|···|2|1 are related to algebras of another combinatorially defined family of Lie subalgebras of sl(n). In particular, p A 1|2|···|2 2|···|2|1 is isomorphic to a type-A Lie poset algebra (see [3]). Type-A Lie poset algebras are subalgebras of sl(n) parametrized by posets on {1, . . . ...\n\nSeaweed algebras and the unimodal spectrum property\n\nPreprint\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Jun 2023\n\n  * Nicholas Mayers\n  * Nicholas Russoniello\n\nIf $\\mathfrak{g}$ is a Frobenius Lie algebra, then the spectrum of $\\mathfrak{g}$ is an algebraic invariant equal to the multiset of eigenvalues corresponding to a particular operator acting on $\\mathfrak{g}$. In the case of Frobenius seaweed subalgebras of $A_{n-1}=\\mathfrak{sl}(n)$, or type-A seaweeds for short, it has been shown that the spectrum can be computed combinatorially using an attendant graph. With the aid of such graphs, it was further shown that the spectrum of a type-A seaweed consists of an unbroken sequence of integers centered at $\\frac{1}{2}$. It has been conjectured that if the eigenvalues are arranged in increasing order, then the sequence of multiplicities forms a unimodal sequence about $\\frac{1}{2}$. Here, we establish this conjecture for certain families of Frobenius type-A seaweeds by finding explicit formulas for their spectra; in fact, for some families we are able to show that the corresponding sequences of multiplicities form log-concave sequences. All arguments are combinatorial.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\n... However, the only Frobenius seaweed subalgebra of sl(4) with the same rank and dimension is p A 4 ({α 3 , α 1 } | {α 3 , α 2 }), but the latter has spectrum g